=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on router2 in its FRR routing-daemon configuration, not in the
physical WAN interface. router2's eth2 (wan_r1_r2, IP 20.20.0.2/8) is fully
UP and carrier-present on both ends; the hardware link is intact. The actual
problem is that during last night's maintenance two files were wiped on router2:
/etc/frr/frr.conf was deleted entirely, and /etc/frr/daemons was left with
"ripd=no". As a result, FRR starts but the RIP daemon never launches ("ripd is
not running" confirmed via vtysh). Without ripd, router2 neither advertises its
connected prefixes (1.0.0.0/8, 128.168.0.0/16) to router1, nor learns the
192.168.x.x department prefixes in return. router2's kernel RIB contains only
its three directly connected routes; any packet arriving from the Server Room
(1.0.0.x) or Internet Lab (128.168.0.x) destined for any 192.168.x.x address
is immediately dropped by router2 with an ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable"
(confirmed by live ping from pc1 to it_hod). The reverse path also fails:
replies from 192.168.x.x hosts travel as far as router2 via router1, but
router2 has no return route and drops them (it_hod → pc1 times out). Router1
still holds stale RIP entries for 1.0.0.0/8 and 128.168.0.0/16 via 20.20.0.2
(expiring in under 90 s), masking the full extent of the break temporarily.
Root cause: router2's FRR configuration and daemons file were accidentally
cleared during maintenance, disabling ripd. Fix: restore /etc/frr/frr.conf with
the correct RIP stanzas (router rip, network statements for eth0/eth1/eth2,
redistribute connected), set ripd=yes in /etc/frr/daemons, and restart FRR
("service frr restart") on router2.
=== END ===
